From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 13:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fingerprint: accept free-form finger names

is_valid_finger_name() in src/fingerprint.c matched the requested name
against valid_finger_names[] and rejected everything else, so the ten
fprintd finger names were the entire namespace the io.FuriOS.Biomd
API would accept. Enroll, RemoveFinger, RenameFinger and
GetFingerEnrollmentDate all sit behind that check.

That vocabulary makes sense on net.reactivated.Fprint, where clients
enroll a named finger and PAM looks it up again, but it is not the only
way a fingerprint UI is built. LuneOS lets the user type a label for
each print and rename it later, which fpd allowed and which biomd
refuses with InvalidArgs before the sensor is ever touched.

Nothing in the daemon needs the name to come from a fixed list. The
template is identified by finger_id; finger_name is a TEXT column that
is only ever read back out and handed to the caller, and
database_add_fingerprint()/database_set_finger_name() take it verbatim.

So make the name a label rather than an enum:

  - database_is_valid_finger_name() keeps only the constraints storage
    and the bus impose - non-empty, UTF-8, at most FINGER_NAME_MAX_BYTES.
    It already accepted anything prefixed "finger_", so the fixed list
    was not an invariant there either.

  - is_valid_finger_name() in fingerprint.c defers to it instead of
    scanning the list a second time. RenameFinger already checked its
    new name with the database rule while checking the old one against
    the list, so the two now agree.

  - valid_finger_names[] stays as what the daemon picks for itself
    (database_get_suggested_finger_name(), the enumerate path) and as
    what ValidFingerNames advertises. It becomes a list of suggested
    names rather than a whitelist; the property keeps its meaning for
    callers that want to offer the fprintd names.

src/fprintd/fprintd.c keeps its own strict copy of the check on
purpose: net.reactivated.Fprint clients expect those ten names and an
InvalidFingername error for anything else, so the bridge must not widen
what it accepts. Its "TODO: this is in fingerprint.c too" note no
longer applies - the two checks differ deliberately now.

One caveat, unchanged from before: RemoveFinger() short-circuits any
name starting with "finger_" by parsing the id out of the name rather
than looking it up. A user-typed "finger_3" therefore still addresses
template 3. Auto-named prints depend on that branch, so it is left
alone.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/include/database.h b/include/database.h
index 5316fd8..4f9ab6b 100644
--- a/include/database.h
+++ b/include/database.h
@@ -13,6 +13,14 @@
 #define DB_DIR "/var/lib/biomd"
 #define DB_PATH "/var/lib/biomd/fingerprints.db"
 
+/*
+ * The names the daemon picks for itself, and the ones it offers through the
+ * ValidFingerNames property. Since a caller may enroll under any label its
+ * user typed, this is a list of suggestions rather than a whitelist - see
+ * database_is_valid_finger_name(). src/fprintd/fprintd.c still treats it as a
+ * whitelist, which is correct there: net.reactivated.Fprint clients expect
+ * these ten names and nothing else.
+ */
 static gchar *valid_finger_names[] = {
     "right-index-finger",
     "left-index-finger",
@@ -27,6 +35,12 @@ static gchar *valid_finger_names[] = {
     NULL
 };
 
+/*
+ * Upper bound on a finger name, in bytes. Names are free-form, but they go
+ * into sqlite and across the bus, so they are not unbounded.
+ */
+#define FINGER_NAME_MAX_BYTES 256
+
 /**
  * Initialize the fingerprint database
  * @return TRUE if successful, FALSE otherwise
diff --git a/src/database.c b/src/database.c
index d48f73c..e6eaf2c 100644
--- a/src/database.c
+++ b/src/database.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <sqlite3.h>
 #include <glib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <time.h>
@@ -464,17 +465,22 @@ database_get_all_fingerprints(void)
 gboolean
 database_is_valid_finger_name(const gchar *finger_name)
 {
+    gsize len;
+
     if (finger_name == NULL)
         return FALSE;
-    if (g_str_has_prefix(finger_name, "finger_"))
-        return TRUE;
 
-    for (int i = 0; valid_finger_names[i] != NULL; i++) {
-        if (g_strcmp0(finger_name, valid_finger_names[i]) == 0)
-            return TRUE;
-    }
+    /*
+     * A finger name is a label, not a key: finger_id is what identifies a
+     * template, the column is plain TEXT, and nothing downstream parses the
+     * name. So accept whatever the user called it and reject only what
+     * sqlite and D-Bus cannot carry - empty, over-long, or not UTF-8.
+     */
+    len = strlen(finger_name);
+    if (len == 0 || len > FINGER_NAME_MAX_BYTES)
+        return FALSE;
 
-    return FALSE;
+    return g_utf8_validate(finger_name, len, NULL);
 }
 
 gchar*
diff --git a/src/fingerprint.c b/src/fingerprint.c
index 5e298a8..300407c 100644
--- a/src/fingerprint.c
+++ b/src/fingerprint.c
@@ -26,18 +26,16 @@ typedef struct {
 
 static BiomFingerprint *fingerprint_state = NULL;
 
+/*
+ * Enroll, RemoveFinger, RenameFinger and GetFingerEnrollmentDate all take the
+ * finger name as a user-visible label, so there is one rule for what a name
+ * may be and database.c owns it. RenameFinger already called that rule for
+ * its new name; going through it here too means the two agree.
+ */
 static gboolean
 is_valid_finger_name(const gchar *finger_name)
 {
-    if (!finger_name)
-        return FALSE;
-
-    for (gint i = 0; valid_finger_names[i] != NULL; i++) {
-        if (g_strcmp0(finger_name, valid_finger_names[i]) == 0)
-            return TRUE;
-    }
-
-    return FALSE;
+    return database_is_valid_finger_name(finger_name);
 }
 
 static gboolean
